{
  "requirement": "Each assessment conversation MUST send its prompt as a system prompt followed by\none user message, the subject message, filled from the first and second text\nblocks of the prompt requirement for the assessment's kind. The kinds are\ntest-adequacy requirement, structural requirement, example, citation review, site\nreview. A system prompt MUST be the same for\nevery assessment of its kind, so every placeholder whose value depends on the\nsubject MUST be in the subject message.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Obligations derived from `requirements.authoring.prompt_parts`: (1) each conversation sends a system prompt followed by one user message, filled from the first and second text blocks of the kind's prompt requirement; (2) this holds for all five kinds (test-adequacy requirement, structural requirement, example, citation review, site review); (3) the system prompt is identical for every assessment of a kind; (4) every subject-dependent placeholder is in the subject message.\n\nCoverage:\n- Obligations 1–2 for all five kinds: `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[subjects_of_one_kind_share_a_byte_identical_system_prompt]` runs `initial_prompt_with_inputs` for two subjects of each kind. The kinds are `initial_prompt` for a requirement, `initial_prompt` for an example, `structural_prompt`, `citation_prompt` and `site_prompt`. Its nested `assert_opening` matches `prompt.system` against block 0 and `prompt.subject` against block 1 of the kind's approved text. It allows only declared placeholders to expand and rejects any leftover `{{`. It also asserts that the subject message starts with `# Subject`.\n- Obligations 1–2 on the wire: `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[initial_prompt_substitutes_subject_and_mandatory_inputs_into_the_template]` runs the CLI for a requirement and an example. It asserts the actual request's system text equals the first block with exact substitutions. It asserts the user message starts and ends with the second block's text around the mandatory inputs. The helper `opening` asserts that message 0 is the system message and message 1 is the user message. The Bedrock and OpenAI request shapes are covered by the same helper.\n- One user message: `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[claude_requests_carry_cache_checkpoints_and_record_cache_usage]` asserts the second request has 3 messages, which implies the first has one. The full template, including the mandatory inputs, must sit inside that single user message, because the test asserts the message begins and ends with the block text. The first request's message count is not asserted directly. I judged this too weak a risk to count as a gap.\n- Obligation 3: `assert_eq!(one.system, two.system)` for two different subjects of each kind, and `assert_ne!(one.subject, two.subject)`.\n- Obligation 4: the system prompt is byte-identical across subjects, and `!one.system.contains(&first.subject_name)` holds. The subject name, acknowledgments, distributed task and mandatory inputs are asserted in the subject message. This is in `a_distributed_requirement_task_continues_with_the_filled_distributed_task`, which shows the distributed task is in the subject message and not the system prompt, and the CLI test above.\n\nThe other marked items are helpers or loosely related tests: `joined`, `prompt_and_tools`, `claude_requests_carry_cache_checkpoints...` and `structural_and_test_reviews_warm_separately...`. They add no gap. No unmarked relevant test is evident. No gaps found.",
